<article>
    <h1>How to split a PDF on iPhone</h1>
    <p>To split a PDF on iPhone, open <a href="/split-pdf">Split PDF</a> in Safari, choose your PDF and enter the page numbers you want to keep. Tap <strong>Split PDF</strong> to download a separate file. For example, enter <code>2-3,7</code> to save the second, third and seventh sheets together. The original stays where you saved it.</p>

    <h2>Save the source PDF where you can find it</h2>
    <p>Open the document and confirm that it is the PDF you want to use. If it is an email attachment or a file open in another app, use that app's share menu to save a copy in Files. Save it somewhere you recognize, such as a folder for the project or a Downloads folder. Then open the copy and note how many sheets show in the viewer. That count helps when the first sheet has a cover and the printed numbering starts later.</p>

    <h2>Extract selected pages in Safari</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Open <a href="/split-pdf">the Split PDF tool</a> and use the file picker to select the PDF you saved.</li>
        <li>Leave <strong>Mode</strong> set to <strong>Extract pages into one PDF</strong>.</li>
        <li>In <strong>Pages</strong>, enter a range with a hyphen and separate additional pages with commas. Type <code>2-3,7</code> for the example above.</li>
        <li>Tap <strong>Split PDF</strong>. The tool creates a new file named <code>extracted-pages.pdf</code>.</li>
        <li>Open the download from Safari or look in your Safari download location in Files. Preview the result before you attach or submit it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Worked example with a seven-sheet packet</h2>
    <p>Suppose you have a cover on sheet 1, a two-page form on sheets 2 and 3, instructions on sheets 4 through 6, and a signature sheet on page 7. You need to send just the form and signature sheet. Enter <code>2-3,7</code>. The new PDF will have three sheets: the first form sheet, the second form sheet and the signature sheet. Check that the new file has three sheets and that the signature is last before sending it. If page 7 appears first in your typed list, the tool still uses the order in the source PDF.</p>

    <h2>Save each sheet as its own download</h2>
    <p>If you need a different file for each sheet, choose <strong>Split every page into its own PDF</strong> instead. A seven-sheet source gives you seven links, named <code>page-1.pdf</code> through <code>page-7.pdf</code>. Download the sheets you need. This mode accepts up to 50 pages. For longer PDFs, use the extract option and choose a smaller range. Both modes accept source files up to 100 MB.</p>

    <h2>When the result contains the wrong page</h2>
    <p>Count from the first sheet in the file, including any cover, blank sheet or contents page. If your report prints “1” after two introductory sheets, the sheet you want is number 3 in the tool. If the range is rejected, check every number against the viewer's total and use plain commas and hyphens. If the PDF requires a password, open or unlock it with the correct password before trying again. The tool processes the file in your browser and leaves the source untouched.</p>

    <h2>Name the copy before sharing it</h2>
    <p>If you extract from several documents, the suggested output name will be the same each time. In Files, give each copy a name that tells you what it contains, such as <code>form-and-signature.pdf</code>. Then open that named copy before sharing. Taking a moment to check the name and page count helps avoid attaching the full packet by mistake.</p>

    <p><a href="/split-pdf">Split your PDF</a> or <a href="/merge-pdf">combine the extracted file with another PDF</a>.</p>
</article>
